from datetime import date, timedelta

def merge(intervals):
    out = []
    for s, e in sorted(intervals):
        if out and s <= out[-1][1]:
            out[-1] = (out[-1][0], e)
        else:
            out.append((s, e))
    return out

def expiry(accrual, years, tolls):
    base = date(accrual.year + years, accrual.month, accrual.day)
    tolled = sum((e - s).days for s, e in merge(tolls))
    return base + timedelta(days=tolled)

Two defects: merge keeps the earlier end when one interval contains another (containment case), and expiry crashes for Feb 29 accruals when accrual.year + years is not a leap year. Write pytest tests that reproduce both — containment intervals (2021-01-01, 2021-12-31) plus (2021-03-01, 2021-06-30) must toll 364 days not more, and accrual 2020-02-29 with years=3 must not raise (invented convention: roll to Feb 28). Watch them fail, fix the code minimally, and get the suite (add two normal-path cases) green.

